{"reference":"Judges 19","book":"Judges","chapter":19,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"The Opening Refrain","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The opening statement that Israel had no king is part of a repeated refrain in the closing narratives. It raises the question of how the lack of a king relates to people doing what seemed right to them, but the refrain alone does not settle what form of kingship, if any, it endorses.","verse_refs":["Judges 19:1"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_019_7ec1b8f94ac59c8846ae8a1f"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Larger Ending of Judges","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The events that follow move into warfare, collective punishment, and forced wife-taking. Together with the sexual violence in this chapter, these acts are presented as signs of social collapse, not as patterns to imitate.","verse_refs":["Judges 20:1-48","Judges 21:1-25"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_019_dd49d83c2ccc50edce36aeeb"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}